1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,886
Session three.

2
00:00:01,166 --> 00:00:02,906
One process, many clients.

3
00:00:03,186 --> 00:00:08,233
The question for this session: how does one box
hold ten thousand clients at the same time?

4
00:00:09,166 --> 00:00:11,209
There are three ways to serve many clients.

5
00:00:11,533 --> 00:00:12,676
Fork per request.

6
00:00:12,956 --> 00:00:15,496
The whole process is copied for each client.

7
00:00:15,776 --> 00:00:19,447
Simple and isolated, but the most
expensive per connection.

8
00:00:19,766 --> 00:00:20,863
Thread per request.

9
00:00:21,143 --> 00:00:23,388
Cheaper than fork, and latency drops.

10
00:00:23,668 --> 00:00:26,799
But every thread still costs a stack
before any work is done.

11
00:00:27,133 --> 00:00:28,684
One loop with many sockets.

12
00:00:28,964 --> 00:00:30,231
With select or epoll,

13
00:00:30,231 --> 00:00:34,756
one thread watches every socket and wakes
only for the ones that are ready.

14
00:00:35,036 --> 00:00:36,454
This is the one that scales.

15
00:00:36,800 --> 00:00:38,416
What actually ships is a mix.

16
00:00:38,696 --> 00:00:41,468
Pre-fork or pre-thread a pool of workers,

17
00:00:41,468 --> 00:00:45,051
and let each worker run its own
select or epoll loop.

18
00:00:45,331 --> 00:00:48,611
Isolation from the processes,
concurrency from the loop.

19
00:00:48,933 --> 00:00:53,413
The cost of fork is a full process image
per connection: page tables,

20
00:00:53,413 --> 00:00:58,672
file descriptors and a scheduler entry
duplicated, all paid while the user waits.

21
00:00:59,599 --> 00:01:00,883
Now the select server.

22
00:01:01,163 --> 00:01:02,127
Here is the loop.

23
00:01:02,466 --> 00:01:06,640
Each iteration, clear the set, add the
server socket, add every client,

24
00:01:06,640 --> 00:01:08,581
and track the highest descriptor.

25
00:01:08,861 --> 00:01:11,311
Then call select with max fd plus one.

26
00:01:11,633 --> 00:01:15,025
If the server socket is marked
ready, accept a new client.

27
00:01:15,666 --> 00:01:17,622
For every client marked ready, read.

28
00:01:18,266 --> 00:01:21,261
If read returns zero or less, close the client,

29
00:01:21,261 --> 00:01:25,149
and delete it in constant time by
swapping in the last entry.

30
00:01:25,429 --> 00:01:26,530
Otherwise, echo.

31
00:01:27,166 --> 00:01:29,213
And note the last argument to select.

32
00:01:29,493 --> 00:01:30,579
That is the timeout.

33
00:01:30,859 --> 00:01:32,252
NULL means block forever.

34
00:01:33,499 --> 00:01:34,893
Why does select hit a wall?

35
00:01:35,233 --> 00:01:39,873
The fd set is a fixed-width bitmap, one
thousand twenty-four bits on glibc.

36
00:01:40,153 --> 00:01:44,551
A descriptor numbered one thousand twenty-
four simply cannot be represented.

37
00:01:45,499 --> 00:01:50,231
The second problem: select is linear in the
number of sockets, every single call.

38
00:01:50,566 --> 00:01:55,432
With select you rebuild the set each iteration,
and the kernel scans every descriptor.

39
00:01:55,712 --> 00:02:00,295
Ten thousand idle sockets cost exactly as
much as ten thousand busy ones.

40
00:02:00,633 --> 00:02:05,197
With epoll, you register each
descriptor once, using epoll_ctl.

41
00:02:05,477 --> 00:02:08,033
Then epoll_wait returns only what fired.

42
00:02:08,313 --> 00:02:10,003
Idle connections cost nothing.

43
00:02:10,333 --> 00:02:13,102
Picture sixty sockets, and only three have data.

44
00:02:13,382 --> 00:02:14,960
select walks all sixty.

45
00:02:15,240 --> 00:02:17,083
epoll hands back just the three.

46
00:02:17,399 --> 00:02:20,198
The number to remember is one
thousand twenty-four.

47
00:02:20,478 --> 00:02:25,797
It is FD_SETSIZE, it is your default
ulimit, and it is the K in C ten K.

48
00:02:26,077 --> 00:02:27,407
One wall, three names.

49
00:02:27,687 --> 00:02:35,021
Other platforms have their own versions of epoll:
kqueue on BSD and macOS, IOCP on Windows,

50
00:02:35,021 --> 00:02:37,358
and io_uring on modern Linux.

51
00:02:38,299 --> 00:02:40,515
In production, you stack these ideas.

52
00:02:40,833 --> 00:02:45,657
At startup, fork or spawn a pool of
workers, often one per CPU core.

53
00:02:45,999 --> 00:02:48,827
Each worker runs its own select or epoll loop.

54
00:02:49,166 --> 00:02:52,813
SO_REUSEPORT lets the kernel spread
new connections across them.

55
00:02:53,133 --> 00:02:57,461
You get crash isolation from the processes,
and concurrency from the loops.

56
00:02:57,799 --> 00:03:03,434
This is Apache's event MPM, nginx's master
and workers, and the Go runtime,

57
00:03:03,434 --> 00:03:09,293
which multiplexes goroutines onto OS threads
with a scheduler on top of epoll.

58
00:03:10,233 --> 00:03:13,275
Timeouts are a contract, and the
client has a half of it.

59
00:03:13,599 --> 00:03:15,208
select waits on three sets.

60
00:03:15,488 --> 00:03:17,943
Read fds: new content arrived.

61
00:03:18,223 --> 00:03:20,889
Write fds: the socket allows writing.

62
00:03:21,169 --> 00:03:26,006
And except fds: sockets being connected,
closed, or raising exceptions.

63
00:03:26,333 --> 00:03:28,226
The last argument is the timeout.

64
00:03:28,506 --> 00:03:32,344
NULL means block forever, so one wedged
client can stop the whole loop.

65
00:03:32,666 --> 00:03:36,814
On the client side, always set a
deadline, and then retry with backoff.

66
00:03:37,094 --> 00:03:39,241
We will come back to that in session seven.

67
00:03:40,166 --> 00:03:42,144
Now, how does the server run your code?

68
00:03:42,424 --> 00:03:46,238
The oldest answer is CGI, the
Common Gateway Interface.

69
00:03:46,566 --> 00:03:50,626
For every request, Apache creates a
brand new CGI process,

70
00:03:50,626 --> 00:03:53,636
with environment variables and three pipes.

71
00:03:53,966 --> 00:03:56,764
The request body goes to the
program's standard in.

72
00:03:57,044 --> 00:04:00,059
Whatever it prints on standard out
becomes the response.

73
00:04:00,339 --> 00:04:02,937
And standard error goes to Apache's error log.

74
00:04:03,266 --> 00:04:05,651
In the real source, mod_cgi dot c,

75
00:04:05,651 --> 00:04:10,841
the function run_cgi_child creates the
child and attaches the three pipes,

76
00:04:10,841 --> 00:04:15,891
with a timeout on each, so a hung script
cannot hold the worker forever.

77
00:04:16,233 --> 00:04:21,093
The cost is that a whole program image is
created, run once, and thrown away,

78
00:04:21,093 --> 00:04:22,355
on every single hit.

79
00:04:23,299 --> 00:04:25,105
How does your program see the request?

80
00:04:25,385 --> 00:04:28,478
HTTP headers become environment variables.

81
00:04:29,499 --> 00:04:31,401
The method becomes REQUEST_METHOD.

82
00:04:31,733 --> 00:04:33,734
The query string becomes QUERY_STRING.

83
00:04:34,066 --> 00:04:39,254
A header like User-Agent: curl becomes
HTTP_USER_AGENT equals curl.

84
00:04:39,534 --> 00:04:43,737
Upper-case, dashes become
underscores, and an HTTP_prefix.

85
00:04:44,066 --> 00:04:46,487
Plus GATEWAY_INTERFACE and SERVER_PROTOCOL.

86
00:04:46,767 --> 00:04:50,342
The code is ap_add_cgi_vars in util_script.c.

87
00:04:50,666 --> 00:04:52,912
The CGI program itself just prints.

88
00:04:53,192 --> 00:04:54,150
Read the method.

89
00:04:54,430 --> 00:04:56,173
For POST, read standard in.

90
00:04:56,453 --> 00:04:58,208
For GET, parse the query string.

91
00:04:58,488 --> 00:05:03,044
Print a Content-Type header, then a blank line,
which is the delimiter, then the body.

92
00:05:03,324 --> 00:05:08,292
Any language that can read environment
variables and print can serve HTTP.

93
00:05:08,572 --> 00:05:10,938
That is why the early web ran on Perl and shell.

94
00:05:11,866 --> 00:05:13,955
Two fixes for the cost of CGI.

95
00:05:14,300 --> 00:05:20,101
One fix is to compile the language into the
server, as mod PHP or mod Perl do.

96
00:05:20,381 --> 00:05:25,616
No new process per request, but the app now shares
an address space with the web server,

97
00:05:25,616 --> 00:05:27,520
so one segfault takes both down.

98
00:05:27,866 --> 00:05:29,820
The fix that won is FastCGI.

99
00:05:30,100 --> 00:05:33,863
Keep a long-lived pool of processes and talk to it

100
00:05:33,863 --> 00:05:37,851
over a Unix or TCP socket using
a reduced vocabulary.

101
00:05:38,131 --> 00:05:42,482
The setup cost is taken off the request
path and paid once at startup,

102
00:05:42,482 --> 00:05:44,346
the same trick as pre-forking.

103
00:05:45,266 --> 00:05:46,698
Here is the architecture.

104
00:05:47,033 --> 00:05:49,197
Thousands of clients, many of them slow.

105
00:05:49,533 --> 00:05:56,082
They talk to nginx or httpd, which is user-
facing: it absorbs the slow connections,

106
00:05:56,082 --> 00:05:58,031
handles TLS, and buffers.

107
00:05:58,366 --> 00:06:01,976
Behind it, you define an
upstream, speaking FastCGI.

108
00:06:02,300 --> 00:06:06,331
To php-fpm: a fixed pool of long-lived workers.

109
00:06:06,666 --> 00:06:11,320
FastCGI is a modified HTTP with less vocabulary.

110
00:06:11,600 --> 00:06:16,843
It carries the same information, method,
headers, query and body, but it is binary,

111
00:06:16,843 --> 00:06:18,232
typed and multiplexed.

112
00:06:19,166 --> 00:06:20,932
Let us look at the actual records.

113
00:06:21,966 --> 00:06:25,260
A request begins with FCGI_BEGIN_REQUEST.

114
00:06:25,600 --> 00:06:32,233
Then FCGI_PARAMS carries the variables:
REQUEST_METHOD equals POST, SCRIPT_FILENAME,

115
00:06:32,233 --> 00:06:35,391
QUERY_STRING, CONTENT_LENGTH, HTTP_HOST.

116
00:06:35,733 --> 00:06:38,795
An empty PARAMS record means the
params are finished.

117
00:06:39,133 --> 00:06:44,928
Then FCGI_STDIN carries the body: name
equals alice and age equals twenty.

118
00:06:45,208 --> 00:06:46,307
Seventeen bytes.

119
00:06:46,633 --> 00:06:50,169
And an empty STDIN record means stdin is finished.

120
00:06:50,500 --> 00:06:56,233
The response comes back as FCGI_STDOUT:
headers, a blank line, and the body,

121
00:06:56,233 --> 00:06:57,817
then an empty STDOUT.

122
00:06:58,166 --> 00:07:03,893
STDERR goes to the log, in its own record type so
debug output can never corrupt the page.

123
00:07:04,173 --> 00:07:05,302
Then END_REQUEST.

124
00:07:05,633 --> 00:07:06,829
The framing is both.

125
00:07:07,109 --> 00:07:10,554
Every record header carries its
content length, and an empty,

126
00:07:10,554 --> 00:07:14,394
length zero record is the delimiter that
says a stream has finished.

127
00:07:15,333 --> 00:07:18,561
The same idea shows up in the
Java world, as servlets.

128
00:07:18,900 --> 00:07:23,728
Jetty and Tomcat are the web server, and the
JVM is already running your code.

129
00:07:24,066 --> 00:07:26,008
A thread pool is created at startup.

130
00:07:26,288 --> 00:07:27,519
That is pre-threading.

131
00:07:27,866 --> 00:07:31,578
Isolation is by class loader and
thread, not by process.

132
00:07:31,858 --> 00:07:35,759
That is cheaper than fork, but a runaway
servlet can hurt its neighbours.

133
00:07:36,100 --> 00:07:41,191
CGI, FastCGI, servlets, and every modern
framework answer one question:

134
00:07:41,191 --> 00:07:46,783
how do we run user code for each request
without creating the world each time?

135
00:07:47,733 --> 00:07:49,236
Is everything a web server?

136
00:07:49,516 --> 00:07:50,325
Not quite.

137
00:07:51,366 --> 00:07:56,046
Plenty of things speak HTTP: a router's
admin page, most Node apps.

138
00:07:56,366 --> 00:07:58,669
Firestore and CouchDB over REST.

139
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:05,770
But Redis speaks RESP, MySQL has its own
binary wire protocol, and MQTT brokers,

140
00:08:05,770 --> 00:08:09,663
messaging backends, and Kafka natively do too.

141
00:08:11,200 --> 00:08:12,763
HTTP is stateless.

142
00:08:13,043 --> 00:08:18,230
Any box can answer any request, a load balancer
can send the next request anywhere,

143
00:08:18,230 --> 00:08:20,543
so horizontal scaling is nearly free.

144
00:08:20,866 --> 00:08:25,599
A database wants to stay connected,
because the TCP connect, handshake,

145
00:08:25,599 --> 00:08:28,732
authentication and session setup are expensive.

146
00:08:29,066 --> 00:08:33,544
So the question becomes: how many
persistent connections can one box hold,

147
00:08:33,544 --> 00:08:35,542
not how many requests per second.

148
00:08:36,466 --> 00:08:38,164
Every server hits four walls.

149
00:08:38,500 --> 00:08:39,998
First, file descriptors.

150
00:08:40,278 --> 00:08:44,728
A connection is a descriptor, and the default
limit is one thousand twenty-four.

151
00:08:45,066 --> 00:08:46,007
Second, memory.

152
00:08:46,287 --> 00:08:50,017
A stack per thread and a buffer per
connection, paid before any work.

153
00:08:50,366 --> 00:08:51,882
Third, context switches.

154
00:08:52,162 --> 00:08:55,724
Every read, write and system call
crosses the kernel boundary.

155
00:08:56,066 --> 00:08:57,184
And fourth, the queue.

156
00:08:57,464 --> 00:08:59,963
Latency and throughput are not independent.

157
00:09:00,243 --> 00:09:01,706
Being slow costs memory.

158
00:09:02,633 --> 00:09:05,009
The history of this problem is two acronyms.

159
00:09:06,333 --> 00:09:09,083
In nineteen ninety-nine, Dan Kegel asked:

160
00:09:09,083 --> 00:09:12,773
can one box hold ten thousand
simultaneous connections?

161
00:09:13,053 --> 00:09:14,744
At the time, the answer was no.

162
00:09:15,066 --> 00:09:19,308
In twenty thirteen, Robert Graham argued
the kernel itself is the problem,

163
00:09:19,308 --> 00:09:22,231
and that you should bypass it
to reach ten million.

164
00:09:22,566 --> 00:09:26,980
Today, two to three million connections have
been demonstrated on one server.

165
00:09:27,260 --> 00:09:30,434
And the default ulimit is still
one thousand twenty-four.

166
00:09:31,366 --> 00:09:32,945
Memory is the second wall.

167
00:09:33,225 --> 00:09:35,503
Compare an OS thread with a goroutine.

168
00:09:35,833 --> 00:09:39,141
An OS thread reserves an eight
megabyte stack up front.

169
00:09:39,421 --> 00:09:43,068
Ten thousand threads is about eighty
gigabytes of address space.

170
00:09:43,400 --> 00:09:47,110
A goroutine starts with a two
kilobyte stack, grown on demand.

171
00:09:47,390 --> 00:09:49,222
That is four thousand times smaller.

172
00:09:49,566 --> 00:09:55,859
The Go runtime multiplexes goroutines onto a few OS
threads with a netpoller built on epoll.

173
00:09:56,139 --> 00:09:59,893
You write simple blocking reads, and the
runtime parks you on a poll.

174
00:10:00,833 --> 00:10:02,936
The third wall is the kernel boundary.

175
00:10:03,266 --> 00:10:06,706
Each trip from user mode into the
kernel and back saves state,

176
00:10:07,033 --> 00:10:08,399
switches page tables,

177
00:10:08,733 --> 00:10:10,054
validates arguments,

178
00:10:10,400 --> 00:10:11,334
copies data,

179
00:10:11,666 --> 00:10:12,556
and returns.

180
00:10:12,900 --> 00:10:15,177
Each one costs hundreds of nanoseconds,

181
00:10:15,177 --> 00:10:18,390
and the Spectre and Meltdown
mitigations made it worse.

182
00:10:18,733 --> 00:10:19,744
Two responses.

183
00:10:20,024 --> 00:10:26,927
Bypass the kernel entirely with user-space
TCP: DPDK, netmap, mTCP, Seastar.

184
00:10:27,207 --> 00:10:30,404
You lose all your usual tools, but
you get the raw packets.

185
00:10:30,733 --> 00:10:31,918
Or make fewer calls.

186
00:10:32,198 --> 00:10:36,220
sendfile moves a file to a socket
without a user-space copy,

187
00:10:36,220 --> 00:10:40,577
and io_uring submits batches of
operations through a shared ring.

188
00:10:41,500 --> 00:10:43,430
Little's Law ties it all together.

189
00:10:43,710 --> 00:10:46,493
Concurrency equals throughput times latency.

190
00:10:46,833 --> 00:10:51,371
Connections in flight equals requests per
second, times seconds per request.

191
00:10:51,700 --> 00:10:56,494
At ten thousand requests per second, with a
latency of one hundred milliseconds,

192
00:10:56,494 --> 00:10:58,532
you hold one thousand connections.

193
00:10:58,866 --> 00:11:03,196
Same traffic, but with a latency of one
second, and you hold ten thousand.

194
00:11:03,533 --> 00:11:05,200
Slowness is a memory bug.

195
00:11:05,480 --> 00:11:11,025
Only getting faster reduces what you hold, so take
high-latency work off the request path,

196
00:11:11,025 --> 00:11:12,566
and do it asynchronously.

197
00:11:13,499 --> 00:11:15,327
Now the servers you actually use.

198
00:11:15,607 --> 00:11:16,651
Apache first.

199
00:11:16,931 --> 00:11:21,318
Its concurrency model is called an MPM,
a Multi-Processing Module,

200
00:11:21,318 --> 00:11:23,245
and it evolved through three.

201
00:11:23,566 --> 00:11:26,679
Prefork: a pool of processes, one connection each.

202
00:11:26,959 --> 00:11:31,178
Bulletproof, works with non-thread-safe
modules, and the most expensive.

203
00:11:31,499 --> 00:11:33,967
Worker: processes each running many threads.

204
00:11:34,247 --> 00:11:39,148
Cheaper, but a thread is pinned to a connection
even while it sits idle on keep-alive.

205
00:11:39,466 --> 00:11:44,491
Event: a listener thread per process parks
idle keep-alive sockets on epoll,

206
00:11:44,491 --> 00:11:47,466
and hands workers only the sockets with work.

207
00:11:47,746 --> 00:11:51,570
It arrived around twenty twelve, and is
the default on Unix today.

208
00:11:52,499 --> 00:11:54,818
HAProxy arrived in two thousand one.

209
00:11:55,166 --> 00:11:56,842
It was largely select at first.

210
00:11:57,122 --> 00:12:02,617
Epoll landed in Linux two point five point
forty-four in late two thousand two,

211
00:12:02,617 --> 00:12:08,669
and HAProxy became multi-threaded only in version
one point eight, in twenty seventeen.

212
00:12:08,999 --> 00:12:12,488
Its big idea was to make the load
balancer a tier of its own,

213
00:12:12,488 --> 00:12:16,034
sitting in front of the app servers
and choosing between them.

214
00:12:16,366 --> 00:12:20,130
A single event-driven process could
saturate a network card,

215
00:12:20,130 --> 00:12:22,890
and it absorbs connection churn by keeping a

216
00:12:22,890 --> 00:12:25,901
pool of persistent connections to the upstreams.

217
00:12:26,833 --> 00:12:31,465
Varnish, from two thousand six, was the
first popular caching reverse proxy.

218
00:12:31,799 --> 00:12:33,330
Squid cached for the client.

219
00:12:33,610 --> 00:12:36,557
It was a forward proxy near the
users, on the way out.

220
00:12:36,837 --> 00:12:40,753
Varnish put the same mechanism in front
of the origin, on the way in.

221
00:12:41,099 --> 00:12:44,146
Internally, an acceptor thread
per listening point,

222
00:12:44,146 --> 00:12:46,655
and a worker thread per active connection,

223
00:12:46,655 --> 00:12:51,612
with pools that default to a minimum of one
hundred and a maximum of five thousand.

224
00:12:51,933 --> 00:12:57,697
Idle descriptors are handed to a waiter, using
epoll on Linux and kqueue on BSD.

225
00:12:57,977 --> 00:13:00,043
Threads for work, a loop for waiting.

226
00:13:00,366 --> 00:13:05,656
Fastly's edge is built on Varnish, and VCL,
the Varnish Configuration Language,

227
00:13:05,656 --> 00:13:09,808
is how you configure that two
thousand six program even today.

228
00:13:10,733 --> 00:13:14,977
And nginx, from two thousand four,
built on epoll and sendfile.

229
00:13:15,299 --> 00:13:19,051
With read and write, bytes go from disk,
to the kernel, to your buffer,

230
00:13:19,051 --> 00:13:21,376
back into the kernel, and out to the socket.

231
00:13:21,656 --> 00:13:25,578
With sendfile, the kernel moves them
straight from the file to the socket.

232
00:13:25,858 --> 00:13:27,199
You never touch the bytes.

233
00:13:27,479 --> 00:13:28,750
That is zero copy.

234
00:13:29,099 --> 00:13:34,621
nginx runs one master process and one
single-threaded worker per CPU core.

235
00:13:34,901 --> 00:13:38,790
Each worker runs one epoll loop over
thousands of connections.

236
00:13:39,133 --> 00:13:44,472
It started as a reverse proxy, and was then
abused for caching, load balancing,

237
00:13:44,472 --> 00:13:46,770
TLS termination and rate limiting.

238
00:13:47,050 --> 00:13:49,092
It became the default shape of an edge.

239
00:13:49,433 --> 00:13:54,207
Since version one point seven point eleven, it
has a thread pool for disk reads,

240
00:13:54,207 --> 00:13:59,637
because a page fault on a cache miss would
otherwise stall every connection on that worker.

241
00:14:00,566 --> 00:14:01,611
Side by side.

242
00:14:02,633 --> 00:14:06,167
Apache, nineteen ninety-five:
prefork, worker, event.

243
00:14:06,447 --> 00:14:10,287
It popularised modules and CGI
to run anyone's code.

244
00:14:10,567 --> 00:14:11,816
Akamai started here.

245
00:14:12,133 --> 00:14:15,437
HAProxy, two thousand one: select then epoll.

246
00:14:15,717 --> 00:14:17,648
The load balancer as its own tier.

247
00:14:17,966 --> 00:14:22,931
Varnish, two thousand six: a thread per
active connection with an epoll waiter.

248
00:14:23,211 --> 00:14:25,957
The caching reverse proxy, now Fastly's edge.

249
00:14:26,299 --> 00:14:30,309
nginx, two thousand four: a master
plus one worker per core.

250
00:14:30,589 --> 00:14:32,847
Sendfile, and the answer to C ten K.

251
00:14:33,166 --> 00:14:37,835
All four ended up in the same place: an event
loop, and a small pool of workers.

252
00:14:38,766 --> 00:14:40,685
Four ideas to keep from this session.

253
00:14:41,033 --> 00:14:41,535
One.

254
00:14:41,815 --> 00:14:42,988
Amortise the setup.

255
00:14:43,268 --> 00:14:46,838
Pre-fork, pre-thread, FastCGI, pools, servlets.

256
00:14:47,166 --> 00:14:47,702
Two.

257
00:14:47,982 --> 00:14:49,609
Nothing is free per connection.

258
00:14:49,889 --> 00:14:54,348
A descriptor, a stack, a buffer and a
scheduler entry, times ten thousand.

259
00:14:54,666 --> 00:14:55,252
Three.

260
00:14:55,532 --> 00:14:57,496
Framing is still length or delimiter.

261
00:14:57,776 --> 00:15:01,842
FastCGI uses a length in each
record, plus an empty record.

262
00:15:02,166 --> 00:15:02,719
Four.

263
00:15:02,999 --> 00:15:05,783
Concurrency equals throughput times latency.

264
00:15:06,063 --> 00:15:08,573
Only getting faster reduces what you hold.

265
00:15:09,499 --> 00:15:12,932
Here is what the quiz is most likely
to ask from session three.

266
00:15:31,466 --> 00:15:32,667
That is session three.

267
00:15:32,947 --> 00:15:36,859
Next up, session four: nginx and
the code that explains it.

